

Today I focused on building my Linux command confidence as part of my DevOps learning journey.

Instead of trying to memorize many commands, I learned a small set of commands that are useful for everyday Linux administration and troubleshooting.

The commands are grouped into:

Process Management
File System
Networking Troubleshooting

I am practicing these commands on an Ubuntu Linux EC2 instance on AWS, connected through SSH using Git Bash.

1. Process Management

A process is a program that is currently running.

Linux assigns every process a unique PID (Process ID).

These commands help us view, search, and manage processes.

ps
ps

Shows currently running processes and their PIDs.

Example:

PID   TTY       TIME     CMD
1998  pts/0     00:00:00 bash
2278  pts/0     00:00:00 ps

Remember: ps gives us a snapshot of running processes.

top
top

Displays running processes and system information such as:

CPU usage
Memory usage
System load
Process information

Unlike ps, top continuously updates the information.

Remember: top is useful for checking what is happening on the server right now.

To exit top, press:

q
pgrep
pgrep <process-name>

Searches for a process by name and displays its PID.

Example:

pgrep systemd

This returned several PIDs on my EC2 instance, including PID 1, which is the main systemd process.

Remember: pgrep = find a process by name.

kill
kill <PID>

Sends a signal to a process, commonly used to terminate it.

For practice, I created a safe temporary process:

sleep 300 &

Then I found its PID and terminated it using:

kill <PID>

Important: Don't randomly kill system processes. Always make sure you know what process you're stopping.

jobs
jobs

Shows background jobs started from the current shell.

For example, when I ran:

sleep 300 &

the process was started in the background.

Remember: jobs = see background jobs from the current terminal.

2. File System Commands

Linux organizes files and directories in a hierarchical filesystem.

These commands help with navigating and managing files.

pwd
pwd

Shows the current working directory.

Example:

/home/ubuntu

Remember: pwd = Where am I?

ls
ls

Lists files and directories in the current location.

Remember: ls = What's here?

cd
cd <directory>

Changes the current directory.

Example:

cd devops

To go one directory back:

cd ..

Remember: cd = Move to another directory.

mkdir
mkdir <directory-name>

Creates a new directory.

Example:

mkdir devops

Remember: mkdir = Create a folder.

touch
touch <file-name>

Creates an empty file.

Example:

touch linux-notes.md

Remember: touch = Create a file.

cp
cp <source> <destination>

Copies a file or directory.

Example:

cp file.txt backup.txt

Remember: cp = Copy.

mv
mv <source> <destination>

Moves a file or directory.

It can also be used to rename files.

Example:

mv old.txt new.txt

Remember: mv = Move or rename.

rm
rm <file>

Removes/deletes a file.

Example:

rm old.txt

Important: Be careful with rm because files normally don't go to a recycle bin.

Remember: rm = Remove.

cat
cat <file>

Displays the contents of a file.

Example:

cat notes.txt

Remember: cat = Display file contents.

less
less <file>

Allows you to read a file page by page.

It is particularly useful for large files.

Remember: less = Read a file comfortably.

head
head <file>

Displays the beginning of a file.

Remember: head = See the beginning.

tail
tail <file>

Displays the end of a file.

A very useful option for DevOps is:

tail -f application.log

This continuously displays new lines added to a log file.

Remember: tail = See the end of a file.

3. Networking Troubleshooting

Networking is very important in DevOps because applications and servers constantly communicate with each other.

Today I learned three basic networking commands.

ping
ping google.com

Checks whether a host is reachable and measures the response time.

Think:

"Can I reach this server?"

To stop ping, press:

Ctrl + C

Remember: ping = Check network reachability.

ip addr
ip addr

Displays network interfaces and their IP addresses.

On my EC2 instance, I saw interfaces such as:

lo
ens5
lo

lo is the loopback interface.

Its address is:

127.0.0.1

This represents the local machine itself.

ens5

ens5 is the main network interface of my EC2 instance.

It had the private IP:

172.31.28.193

Remember: ip addr = Show network interfaces and IP addresses.

curl
curl https://example.com

Sends a request to a URL and displays the server's response.

For example, it can be used to check:

Whether a website is responding
Whether an API is working
Whether an HTTP service is reachable
What response a server is returning

A common DevOps example is:

curl http://localhost:8080

This can be used to test an application running locally on port 8080.

Remember: curl = Communicate with and test web servers/APIs.

